Z||000000||87FieroGT||11-27-2000||12:29 AM||||Is there a difference between the clutch pressure plate for an Isuzu 5 Speed transmission and a Muncie 5 Speed?  Someone please let me know ASAP. Thanks.||||reg||13
Z||000001||mrfiero||11-27-2000||12:48 AM||||Yes....the are not interchangeable.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||11-27-2000||05:28 AM||||Yes, I have no bananas.<P>This is news to me. What are the differences in the preasure plates? For more holding power and because I could get one for a good deal, I had Fiero Motorsports put in a V6 clutch, preasure plate and bearing onto a 87 coupe 5 speed. It was great (even at the drag races) all the way up to the day it was totaled by a truck slamming it from behind.<P>The books will say they are different but I could never see the differences between the two other than size. One would reason that you would not want to put a 4 cylinder clutch onto a V6 due to holding power. But why can't you go the other way around? <P>I had a great time with my "upgrade."||||reg||5
Z||000003||mrfiero||11-27-2000||11:57 AM||||I've always been told that they're different, so forgive me.  I guess going from a 4-cyl. to a V-6 pressure plate would work, but I'm with you.....I wouldn't got the other way with it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||11-27-2000||12:41 PM||||The only difference is the throwout bearing. The clutch will fit and so will the pressure plate. Other differences might be the flywheel from a four cylinder to a six cylinder.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
